AB  - We present MoonLynq, a mobile wireless network link emulation framework based on dynamic channel properties generated from a 3D virtual world model. While scriptable packet processors like MoonGen make it possible to build complex network link emulators, a more realistic virtual world model is required to generate the many network parameters based on the (virtual) physical environment. To address this, we integrate a MoonGen-based link emulator with a virtual physical world built in Unity, with PHY and MAC layer emulation adapted from or inspired by INET, Simu5G, and GEMV2. The system implements essential link-layer behaviors of 5G including PHY (Physical) layer throughput control, an RLC (Radio Link Control) transmission buffer, and HARQ (Hybrid Automatic Repeat Request) retransmissions in MoonGen scripts. By modeling LoS (Line-of Sight) and NLoS (Non-Line-of Sight) propagation, including mechanisms like obstacle loss, path loss, reflections and diffractions attenuation, the system generates realistic network parameters that enable more realistic emulation of wireless communication.
